course: dictionary
lesson: 13748
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word interference is a good example. «Nobody asked for Mum's interference in our game» is about getting involved in someone else's business when you are not wanted, so here it means meddling.
explain: «Static interference made the radio crackle and hiss» is about a signal. The clues are radio, crackle and hiss: interference means noise that spoils a signal. Ask yourself which meaning fits the words around it, then test that meaning in the sentence.
explain: In sport, «The referee gave a free kick for interference» means a player wrongly blocked another. Same word, different world. When the context is unclear, check the dictionary and pick the meaning that makes sense.
fact: Some words have several meanings, and the context shows which one is used.
fact: Interference can mean getting involved in someone else's business when you are not wanted.
fact: Interference can mean noise or crackle that spoils a radio, TV or phone signal.
fact: In sport, interference is a foul when a player wrongly blocks another.
fact: In science, interference is when waves combine and add up or cancel out.
exercise: «Nobody asked for her interference in our plans.» What does interference mean here? | Waves that cancel each other out | *Getting involved when you are not wanted | Noise on a phone line | A foul in a football match
exercise: «Static interference made the radio crackle.» What does interference mean? | Waves that add up | A foul in a game | *Noise that spoils a signal | Getting involved in someone's business
exercise: «The referee gave a free kick for interference.» What does interference mean here? | A kind of football boot | *A wrong block by a player | A goal scored by the team | A loud cheer from the crowd
test: «Two waves met and cancelled each other out.» In science, what is interference? | Getting involved in someone's business | *Waves combining and adding up or cancelling out | A foul in sport | A crackling noise on the radio
test: Which sentence uses interference to mean noise? | *«Static interference spoiled the phone call.» | «The player wrongly blocked her.» | «The waves were big and tall.» | «The teacher's interference annoyed us all.»
exam: «Without interference from the crowd, the singer finished her song.» What does without interference mean here? | With a loud noise from the crowd | After a foul in the game | Where two waves cancel out | *Without anyone getting in the way or spoiling it
